- Mechanics of Classes -

-> Object Life Cycle

We have previously talked about object instantiation - creating a new object of some class. A closer look at the 
complete life cycle of an object can provide some helpful insights.

First, let's make a clear distinction between a variable and an object. We know that all variables of a non-primitive 
type are reference variables - they store a reference to an object or a null reference. While it is reasonable to say 
that an int variable is an int (because an int variable, being a value type variable, will always store some int value), 
it is not safe to say the same kind of thing for a reference variable.

Imagine that we have a simple Bunny class, and we then declare a variable of type Bunny like this.
	Bunny flopsy;

In this case, the value stored in the variable flopsy is a null reference. In other words flopsy is not a Bunny it's a 
reference (to nothing in this case).

Even if we instantiate a Bunny object and assign that to the variable,
	flopsy = new Bunny();

It is still important to realize that flopsy is not a Bunny; it is a reference to a Bunny object. The variable flopsy 
is still a name that refers to a location in computer memory where a value is stored, but the value stored in the 
variable flopsy is a reference (to a Bunny object), and not a Bunny object itself.

** An object and the variable that refers to it,  are two different things. **

This distinction may seem small and insignificant; however, a clear understanding of the difference between objects and 
the variables that refer to them will enable you to design, write, test, and debug object oriented solutions in code.

Remember the example (below) from a previous unit. Running this code results in the creation of two reference variables 
(flopsy and cottontail) and the instantiation of one Bunny object. By the time the code is finished running, both 
variables refer to the same object.

	CODE
01 Bunny flopsy;
02 Bunny cottontail;
03
04 flopsy = new Bunny();
05 flopsy.name = "Flopsy";
06 flopsy.age = 8;
07
08 cottontail = flopsy;
09 cottontail.name = "Cottontail";
10 cottontail.age = 5;
11
12 System.out.println(flopsy.name);
	

	MEMORY
flopsy     : <Bunny object 1>
cottontail : <Bunny object 1>

<Bunny object 1> 
