Now that we have made the distinction between reference variables and objects, we can explore the life cycle of an 
object. We will use the following code as the basis for this discussion.

01 class Bunny {
02     public String name = "";
03     public int age = 0;
04
05     public Bunny() {
06         // a default constructor that does nothing
07     }
08
09     public Bunny(String name, int age) {
10         this.name = name;
11         this.age = age;
12     }
13
14     public void hop() {
15         System.out.print(this.age + " year old " + this.name);
16         System.out.println(" hops around.");
17    }
18 }
19
20 public class Main {
21     public static void main(String []args) {
22         Bunny flopsy = new Bunny("Flopsy", 8);
23         flopsy.hop();
24     }
25 }

The life cycle of the Bunny object that will be created and used in the above code example is composed of six steps:
  1.  Instantiation
  2.  Initialization
  3.  Construction
  4.  Assignment
  5.  Use
  6.  Destruction

Steps 1 through 4 all occur by executing line 22 and these steps finish before line 22 completes; so there is quite a 
lot going on on line 22. Let's dive into some of the details of each step.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

					Step 1: Instantiation

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Instantiation occurs on line 22 in the above code new Bunny("Flopsy", 8). The keyword new followed by a class 
constructor call causes a new object of the class type to be created (instantiated) in the computer's memory. 
The computer can actually be quite efficient about this. It only needs to make a space in memory big enough to hold the 
object's variables. For a Bunny object, this is just a String variable called name, and an int variable called age. So, 
in simple terms a Bunny object in memory is just a String called name and an int called age.

In the diagram below, we can see that after the Bunny object has been instantiated, there is a Bunny object in memory, 
and there is a Flopsy variable in memory that currently stores a null reference

		MEMORY 

flopsy : null

<Bunny object 1> 
    name :  
    age : 0

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
						
						Step 2: Initialization

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Immediately after the object's variables are placed in memory, the variables are initialized to the values supplied by 
initializer expressions or to default values. The initializer expression for a Bunny's name variable 
(public String name = ""; on line 02) causes a Bunny's name to be initialized to an empty String. The initializer 
expression on line 03 (public int age = 0;) causes a Bunny's age to be initialized to 0.

After initialization, we can see that the Bunny object's variables (name and age) now store the initialization values.

	MEMORY 

flopsy : null

<Bunny object 1> 
    name :  ""
    age : 0

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

						Step 3:  Construction

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Immediately after the object's variables are initialized a constructor is called. If there is no constructor explicitly 
defined in the class, then a default constructor with no body of code is called. Otherwise one of the explicit 
constructors is called based on which constructor's parameters match (by number and type) the arguments provided in the 
constructor call.

In the example above, the constructor with a String parameter and an int parameter is being called. The values "Flopsy", 
and 8 are being passed as arguments to be stored in the constructor's parameter variables name and age. And, these in 
turn, are being used to store values in the Bunny object's instance variables name and age.

We can see that after the constructor call is finished, the Bunny object's variables now store the values passed as 
arguments to the Bunny constructor method.

		MEMORY 

flopsy : null

<Bunny object 1> 
    name :  "Flopsy"
    age : 8

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

						Step 4: Reference Assignment

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Once the constructor returns, the expression new Bunny("Flopsy", 8) evaluates to a reference to this newly 
instantiated, initialized, and constructed Bunny object, and that reference is stored in the variable flopsy. 
At this point line 22 is finally finished executing, and we can move on to line 23.

Note, in the diagram below, that the flopsy variable is no longer null; it now stores a reference to <Bunny object 1> 
object in memory.

	MEMORY 

flopsy : <Bunny object 1>

<Bunny object 1> 
    name : "Flopsy"
    age : 8

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

						Step 5: Object Use

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

On line 23 (flopsy.hop();) we are using the Bunny object we created on line 22. We are using the variable flopsy 
(a reference to this Bunny object) and dot notation to access and call the hop method. This is the only use we have for 
this object in the example program, but you could continue to use the object as long and often as you like.

	MEMORY 

flopsy : <Bunny object 1>

<Bunny object 1> 
    name : "Flopsy"
    age : 8

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

						Step 6: Object Destruction

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

After line 23 finishes, the main method will return. When a method returns, its local variables are destroyed. So, when 
main returns, the local variable flopsy will be destroyed. At this point there are no references to the Bunny object 
that we created on line 22.

	MEMORY 

<Bunny object 1> 
    name : "Flopsy"
    age : 8

When there are no remaining references to an object, the object will be destroyed - the memory used to store its 
variables will be freed up for other uses.

